<article class="article-body">

<p>The instinct to promote someone you already trust into a chief of staff role makes sense on the surface: they already know the business, the culture, and you. It's also the decision I've seen backfire most often, not because internal candidates are weak, but because the two paths solve genuinely different problems and founders frequently pick based on comfort rather than fit.</p>

<h2>What an internal promotion actually gets you</h2>
<p>Context, immediately. Someone promoted internally already understands the product, the team dynamics, and the history behind decisions that would take an external hire months to absorb. Trust is also already established in both directions, which matters for a role that needs access to sensitive information from day one. The tradeoff is that an internal candidate often lacks the operating frameworks, the dashboards, the decision-log discipline, the meeting cadence design, that someone who's done this specifically before brings as a default, not something they have to invent under pressure.</p>

<h2>What an external hire actually gets you</h2>
<p>A chief of staff who's run this playbook elsewhere brings systems that already work, instead of systems you're hoping they'll figure out. They also carry no internal political baggage, which matters more than founders expect: an internal promotion can create resentment among peers who now report into someone they used to sit next to, and the newly promoted person has to navigate that while also learning a job they've never done. The tradeoff is ramp time on context, and a real cost if the fit turns out to be wrong.</p>

<h2>The question that actually decides it</h2>
<p>Not "who do I trust more," but "does this company need operating systems built, or does it need someone to run systems that already exist?" If the honest answer is that nothing resembling a decision log, an SLA dashboard, or a real meeting cadence exists yet, you're not hiring a chief of staff, you're hiring someone to build operational infrastructure from scratch, and that's a different skill than being well-liked and available.</p>

<h2>A middle path worth considering</h2>
<p>Some of the strongest setups I've seen pair an external fractional chief of staff with an internal successor: the external person builds the systems and runs the first six to twelve months, deliberately documenting as they go, and an internal person shadows with the intent of eventually taking it over. It gets the systems built by someone who's done it before, and it gets the long-term continuity of someone who already has the company's trust.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before deciding</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Do operating systems already exist</strong>, or does someone need to build them from nothing?</li>
  <li><strong>Can the internal candidate absorb a new political dynamic</strong> with former peers reporting to them, on top of learning the role itself?</li>
  <li><strong>Is there time to let an external hire ramp</strong>, or is the need urgent enough that internal context matters more than frameworks?</li>
  <li><strong>Would a hybrid, external build-out with an internal successor, actually serve the company better than either extreme?</strong></li>
</ul>

